---
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import Example from '../../components/Example.astro';
const base=import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const example=`<link rel="stylesheet" href="reva.css">
<link rel="stylesheet" href="reva.glass.css">

<section data-material="glass"
         data-theme="dark"
         data-tone="cool"
         data-shape="rounded"
         data-density="comfortable"
         data-depth="pronounced">
  <article class="card">
    <h2>A frosted surface</h2>
    <p>Your content stays in native HTML.</p>
    <button data-accent="teal">Primary action</button>
  </article>
  <section data-material="solid">
    <article class="card">An opaque local reset</article>
  </section>
</section>`;
---
<DocsLayout title="Glass material" active="glass">
<link rel="stylesheet" href={base+'reva/reva.glass.css'}/>
  <h1>Glass material</h1>
  <p class="prose">An optional frosted surface extension for cards, navigation, standalone disclosures, fieldsets, dialogs and popovers. It adds no runtime JavaScript and leaves the core stylesheet unchanged.</p>
  <div class="row"><a class="button" href={base + 'preview/glass/light/'}>Light preview</a><a class="button secondary" href={base + 'preview/glass/dark/'}>Dark preview</a><a href={base + 'preview/glass/auto/'}>Device theme preview</a></div>
  <h2>Install and activate</h2><p>Load the extension after the core. <code>data-material="glass"</code> applies the treatment to supported surfaces in that section. <code>data-material="solid"</code> ends it locally. A nested glass attribute starts another scope. <code>data-theme</code> retains its existing light, dark and auto values.</p>
  <Example id="glass-install" title="Stylesheet order" description="Copy both stylesheets from dist into your project. Load core first, then the optional material." render={false} html={'<link rel="stylesheet" href="reva.css">\n<link rel="stylesheet" href="reva.glass.css">'}/>
  <Example id="glass-surface" title="Glass with a local solid reset" description="The same shared attributes control this dark, cool surface. The nested solid section remains opaque." glass html={example.slice(example.indexOf('<section')).replace('<button data-accent="teal">', '<button type="button" data-accent="teal">')}/>
  <Example id="glass-contrast" title="Opaque high contrast glass" description="Set data-contrast more to remove translucency and blur locally." glass html={'<section data-material="glass" data-contrast="more">\n  <article class="card"><h3>Clear content</h3><p>An opaque fallback using the current theme.</p></article>\n</section>'}/>
  <Example id="glass-reentry" title="Restart glass inside a solid section" description="A new glass attribute creates another local material scope." glass html={'<section data-material="glass">\n  <section data-material="solid">\n    <article class="card">Opaque surface</article>\n    <article class="card" data-material="glass">Glass again</article>\n  </section>\n</section>'}/>
  <p>For scoped use, load <code>reva.scoped.css</code> followed by <code>reva.glass.scoped.css</code> and put the component inside a <code>.reva</code> boundary. Use matching global or scoped builds.</p>
  <h2>Shared attributes</h2>
  <div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Glass attribute support" tabindex="0">
    <table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Attribute</th><th scope="col">Glass behavior</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row"><code>data-theme</code>, <code>data-tone</code></th><td>Choose the existing colour mode and surface palette, independently of material.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row"><code>data-accent</code></th><td>Colours semantic buttons and native accent controls. Ordinary navigation text keeps its existing link/text tokens.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row"><code>data-shape</code>, <code>data-density</code>, <code>data-type</code></th><td>Keep the existing rounding, component spacing and text sizing.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row"><code>data-depth</code></th><td>Controls surface and dropdown shadows; flat removes them.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row"><code>data-border</code></th><td>Controls decorative surface frames, including desktop dropdowns. Form-control boundaries remain visible.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row"><code>data-fill</code></th><td>Gradient adds a subtle glass sheen and keeps button gradients. Solid removes the sheen; use material solid to remove translucency.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row"><code>data-controls</code></th><td>Native inputs keep their styled/minimal treatments. Filled buttons and styled fields stay opaque for clarity.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row"><code>data-motion</code></th><td>Existing control motion remains governed by the shared setting. Glass introduces no animation.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row"><code>data-contrast</code></th><td>More makes glass opaque and removes blur. Auto restores the material default unless a system preference requests less transparency or more contrast.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Component attributes</th><td>Size, width, table, image and gap settings also inherit from html or a section and affect their documented targets. A nearer attribute overrides only its own setting.</td></tr>
    </tbody></table>
  </div>
  <h2>Readable defaults and fallbacks</h2>
  <p class="prose">The default glass backing is 90% of the current surface colour with 16px backdrop blur. The optional <code>--re-glass-opacity</code> token accepts a percentage; reducing it or supplying custom colours requires contrast checks against the actual background. Blur does not guarantee readability. Decorative borders are not substitutes for form-control boundaries.</p>
  <p class="prose">Browsers without backdrop filtering retain opaque core surfaces. Reduced-transparency and increased-contrast preferences remove translucency and blur where the browser supports those media queries. Forced colours use system colours, and printed surfaces are opaque. An explicit <code>data-contrast="more"</code> is also available.</p>
  <h2>Navigation and overlays</h2><p class="prose">The top menu and desktop dropdown panels support glass; nested navigation disclosures keep their transparent layout. Interaction, touch targets and responsive behavior remain the native top-menu contract. Dialog and popover surfaces can be glass, but their opening behavior stays native HTML. Avoid stacking many blurred surfaces or placing essential text directly on a decorative backdrop. An ancestor with opacity, filters, clipping or stacking constraints can change the blur or overlay result.</p>
  <p class="prose">Automated checks cover the shipped examples and attribute behavior. Glass does not certify a consuming page: actual imagery, custom palettes, screen readers and real devices still need review before release. npm publication remains on hold.</p>
</DocsLayout>
